\subsubsection inst_vs_include 2.4.4 #include

In all cases you need to add the following directive to your project's
source file:
\verbatim
#include <oh/objecthandler.hpp>
\endverbatim

\subsubsection inst_vs_link 2.4.5 Link

Your project's link to the %ObjectHandler library is established
automatically by the directive
\verbatim
#pragma comment(lib, OBJHANDLER_LIB_NAME)
\endverbatim
in file <tt>oh/autolink.hpp</tt>

The name of the library depends on your compiler and configuration.
For example, if you're using VS 2026 (v145), platform x64, configuration
Release (static runtime), the library is called
\verbatim
ObjectHandler-v145-x64-mt-s-1_42_0.lib
\endverbatim

The location of the library depends on which project you're using
(ohlib/ohxllib/ohxll), please consult the tables above.

\section inst_cmake 3 CMake

The cmake build runs on both Windows and POSIX systems (e.g. Linux).  On POSIX
systems it builds the C++ library (<tt>ohlib</tt>) and the C++ example; on
Windows it additionally builds the Excel components.  Each platform has its own
presets - the example below uses the Linux preset <tt>linux-gcc</tt>; on Windows
you would select one of the <tt>windows-...</tt> presets instead.  To see the
presets available on your platform, run <tt>cmake --list-presets</tt> from the
%ObjectHandler directory (only presets whose host condition matches your current
operating system are listed).

\subsection inst_cmake_download 3.1 Download

\subsubsection inst_cmake_directories 3.1.1 Directories

This example assumes that you do not have root access to your machine, so that
the entire build and installation environment resides under your home
directory.

Let's say your home directory is <tt>/home/username</tt>.  Create a directory
where you will unpack and compile the code.  For purposes of this tutorial it
is:
\verbatim
/home/username/build
\endverbatim

\subsubsection inst_cmake_uncompress 3.1.2 Uncompress the Source Code

Download the source-code distribution archive from:

<tt>&lt;download-url&gt;</tt>

and uncompress it under <tt>/home/username/build</tt>.  This single archive contains
gensrc, %ObjectHandler and the other projects in the distribution, already laid
out side by side under a single top-level <tt>QuantLibAddin</tt> folder; no
separate per-project download is required.  After unpacking you will have, among
others:
\verbatim
/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin/gensrc
/home/username/build/QuantLibAddin/ObjectHandler
\endverbatim

\subsection inst_cmake_build 3.2 Build

The %ObjectHandler cmake build is driven by presets.  Configure and build the
library and example with:

\verbatim
cd /home/username/build/QuantLibAddin/ObjectHandler
cmake --preset linux-gcc -DRUN_GENSRC=ON
cmake --build --preset linux-gcc
\endverbatim

The <tt>-DRUN_GENSRC=ON</tt> option runs gensrc (a Python 3 script) to
regenerate the auto-generated source code; it is required for the first build
from a fresh release.  On POSIX systems the build produces the core C++ library
<tt>ohlib</tt>; the Excel components are built only on Windows.

